---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import CodeGroup from "../../../components/CodeGroup.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import { pickAndChooseCss } from "../../../utils/getting-started"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships first-class React 19 components. Install the package, import what
    you need, and you're set.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="installation">Install via NPM</h2>
    <p>
      The components ship as uncompiled TypeScript (<code>.tsx</code> and <code
        >.ts</code
      >), so your build has to compile them. They need React 19 or later. Vite
      with <code>@vitejs/plugin-react</code> and Astro with <code
        >@astrojs/react</code
      > compile them as they are. In Next.js, add <code
        >transpilePackages: ["opui-css"]</code
      > to <code>next.config</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeGroup
      tabs={[
        {
          title: "pnpm",
          code: "pnpm add opui-css open-props react react-dom -S",
          lang: "sh",
        },
        {
          title: "npm",
          code: "npm install opui-css open-props react react-dom -S",
          lang: "sh",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="css">Import the CSS</h2>
    <p>
      React components ship markup only - the CSS still has to be imported once,
      in your root layout or the entry file that renders your app. Import
      everything:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.tsx
import "opui-css/css/imports.css"`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
    <p>
      Or pick and choose in a CSS file, and import that file in your entry
      instead:
    </p>
    <Code code={`/* src/styles/main.css */\n${pickAndChooseCss}`} lang="css" />
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.tsx
import "./styles/main.css"`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
    <p>
      Import <code>layers.css</code> first and <code>utils.css</code> last, it holds
      <code>.ui-sr-only</code> and the motion classes. Add <code
        >choice.css</code
      > with <code>checkbox.css</code>, <code>radio.css</code> or <code
        >switch.css</code
      >. Import <code>opui-css/css/components.css</code> instead of single component
      files to get every component.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="usage">How to use</h2>
    <Code
      code={`import { Button } from "opui-css/react"

export default function App() {
  return <Button variant="filled">Click me</Button>
}`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
    <p>
      Containers take parts, such as <code>CardHeader</code> and <code
        >DialogActions</code
      >. Other slots are props: the default slot is <code>children</code> and named
      slots are camelCased, such as <code>endText</code>. Form controls take the
      native
      <code>value</code> or <code>defaultValue</code>, <code>checked</code> or <code
        >defaultChecked</code
      >, and <code>onChange</code> props.
    </p>
    <p>
      <DocLink href="/guide/concepts">Concepts</DocLink> explains how props map to
      classes, the cascade layers and what needs JavaScript. Then browse the <DocLink
        href="/components">components</DocLink
      >. <DocLink href="/guide/theming">Theming</DocLink> covers colors, density,
      motion and contrast.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="ssr">Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Components that need context, state or effects (<DocLink
        href="/components/anchor">Anchor</DocLink
      >, <DocLink href="/components/callout">Callout</DocLink>, <DocLink
        href="/components/dialog">Dialog</DocLink
      >, <DocLink href="/components/drawer">Drawer</DocLink>, <DocLink
        href="/components/form">Field group</DocLink
      >, <DocLink href="/components/tabs">Tabs</DocLink>, <DocLink
        href="/components/toggle">Toggle group</DocLink
      >, <DocLink href="/components/tooltip">Tooltip</DocLink> and the form controls)
      are marked <code>"use client"</code>. The rest also render as React Server
      Components.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without hydration (Astro with <code>@astrojs/react</code> and no <code
        >client:*</code
      > directive, or <code>renderToString</code> on its own), every component renders
      complete HTML. The native controls work and submit with their form. A few things
      only update on the client:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/checkbox#indeterminate">Checkbox</DocLink>
        <code>indeterminate</code>: it's a DOM property, so the box looks
        unchecked. Or call <code>activateIndeterminate()</code> from <code
          >opui-css/css/js/checkbox.js</code
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/range#value">Range</DocLink> value: the <code
          >&lt;output&gt;</code
        > doesn't follow the thumb. The track fill is CSS, so it's fine.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hydrate those (<code>client:load</code> in Astro) or render them on the client.
      Component pages say what needs hydration next to the browser support chips,
      and the API tables mark each prop with a crossed-out server icon.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
